Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Infra Analysis", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Abstract written by Pavel Hajek on 26 October 2026.", \
"TI"]], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The main component of InfraAnalysis is ",
 StyleBox["InfraCalculus",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", which develops integration and differentiation of functions on the \
vertices of directed acyclic graphs. It is a ",
 StyleBox["mesoscale theory",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". A function corresponds to labels on vertices. We can imagine these labels \
as aggregations on a more elementary unlabeled graph, for example by counting \
the number of elements in the fibers of some observer-induced fibration. ",
 StyleBox["InfraIntegration",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " takes several forms of aggregation along the natural direction from \
sources to sinks. ",
 StyleBox["InfraDerivative",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " is an operation on labeled directed graphs chosen such that, together with \
the corresponding integration, it satisfies the ",
 StyleBox["fundamental theorem of calculus",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". That is, differentiation is a left inverse of infra integration. \
Interestingly, some derivatives are non-local."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A directed graph can be seen as a fibered graph over a path graph, where \
the fibration map is given by the graph distance to sources. The \
decomposition into horizontal and vertical components gives a decomposition \
into ",
 StyleBox["transverse and radial subgraphs",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". We can consider various notions of blow-ups, either in the radial or \
transverse directions. These can be parametrized by a group parameter and \
induce an action on functions on the graph, giving a ",
 StyleBox["renormalization flow",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". We then ask about the ",
 StyleBox["renormalizability of infra calculus",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We have several methods of ",
 StyleBox["coordinatization",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", meaning assigning tuples of labels to graph vertices. One example is the \
shortest-path projection onto a collection of orthogonal paths, as in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["OrthogonalCoordinates"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/OrthogonalCoordinates", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "e01e1b97-84ec-4d39-9c4f-16ae686d76ec"],
 ". This makes the graph fibered over a coordinate grid. A linear order on \
the individual coordinate components then defines directed acyclic graphs \
through the points. We can use these to define ",
 StyleBox["partial derivatives",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", leading to ",
 StyleBox["multivariate calculus",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". We study the validity of ",
 StyleBox["Stokes' and Green's theorems",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We also consider various versions of ",
 StyleBox["differential forms",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " on a graph and study their relations to cochains on the Rips complex. ",
 StyleBox["Tangent and cotangent graphs",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", connections, and ",
 StyleBox["covariant derivatives",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " are also parts of InfraAnalysis."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

